#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068918 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068570 :question/id #uuid "03e461a3-0fe0-5fb5-813e-ae23a5dc606a" :question/math-academy-id "e-467" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/4} \\dfrac{\\tan x \\sec^2 x}{1+\\tan^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u = 1+\\tan^2 x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86e493cf-4eca-5e4c-80d2-77afb41f75fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 + \\sqrt{2} - \\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065452} {:answer/id #uuid "9165ce54-93c6-51e3-8558-20f309c69be9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (1 + \\sqrt{2}) - \\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065453} {:answer/id #uuid "0344248e-4755-5685-a633-05cc9a0872eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/val