db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{6}^{x/3}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295950} {:answer/id #uuid "cd384b9e-3f21-5c93-8eef-f209d288044d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\cdot {6}^{x/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296343} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ca43b4-033c-5cd0-a8a6-8ea7eb292ebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\cdot {6}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296410} {:answer/id #uuid "e19fa8a4-2ede-5cc9-a74c-dbb6cdba1380" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{6}^{x/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd384b9e-3f21-5c93-8eef-f209d288044d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\cdot {6}^{x/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296343} :answer-field/id #uuid "1db9f319-6a3e-51d1-bbc2-fd789260b0da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296690}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a2ea8a4-727a-5496-81cd-8ee505de6239" :question/math-academy-id "q-31417" :question/problem "$\\int {6}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{3},$ then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {6}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {6}^{u}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int {6}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\frac{{6}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {6}^{x/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9887105-989f-5006-a00d-02997b258f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{108}{\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9887105-989f-5006-a00d-02997b258f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{108}{\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318395} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4926046-d89b-5f97-a176-004edee262b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318786}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94f9e06c-ebba-5efe-8b0d-89981a71a1e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-349625" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}{3}^{7-2x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=7-2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{3}^{7-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{5}^{3}{3}^{u}(-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{3}{3}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}){|}_{5}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{3}-{3}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(27-243) \\\\  & =\\frac{108}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cabb7ef7-a5b4-56a3-899b-b57c3c0202b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cabb7ef7-a5b4-56a3-899b-b57c3c0202b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318237} :answer-field/id #uuid "b60ea954-3843-5ca8-a4a4-8f56cec4f3ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318818}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84ff8262-4ace-5df5-9e7e-b8458e5e65ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-349626" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}3\\cdot {2}^{5-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=5-x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,-\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}3\\cdot {2}^{5-x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{2}3\\cdot {2}^{u}(-1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{4}^{2}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3(\\frac{{2}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}){|}_{4}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{2}-{2}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(4-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1e0d6d6-2adc-5995-80ee-179a5a470810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1e0d6d6-2adc-5995-80ee-179a5a470810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318207} :answer-field/id #uuid "7afa991a-6722-56e2-897c-813ea6c1aef9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318684}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efb81182-b47b-5e4a-b191-d85df24ba0ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-349631" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\cdot {4}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=1-x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,-\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\cdot {4}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}2\\cdot {4}^{u}(-1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{1}^{0}{4}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{{4}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}){|}_{1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({4}^{0}-{4}^{1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(1-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "723152d6-ad7a-5796-b242-18a007f91b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{5\\operatorname{ln}5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "723152d6-ad7a-5796-b242-18a007f91b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{5\\operatorname{ln}5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317958} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ca831c-f339-532f-9ae7-145293aca3d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318616}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "442a9420-19fe-5c7f-9765-ddb48edbec27" :question/math-academy-id "q-349646" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}{5}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=x-1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{5}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{5}^{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}({5}^{1}-{5}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(5-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd67e3a7-28e0-5d80-ab9d-4eee46bad316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd67e3a7-28e0-5d80-ab9d-4eee46bad316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318412} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f378587-0bf1-5acc-baa3-21eda4addc7b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318771}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ff1abc0-7cbc-5bd4-915a-56c8cc58e75d" :question/math-academy-id "q-349648" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{3}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=x+1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{3}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{3}^{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{2}-{3}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(9-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09ca6815-86fe-5f83-92e3-96c606c3263e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6^{\\frac{x}{5}}}{\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493007} {:answer/id #uuid "6e14030d-94e3-5db6-a373-8aeb4879a2bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6^{\\frac{x}{5}}}{5 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493013} {:answer/id #uuid "73e0c2e2-7fd4-564c-8dd3-10b1aa2a7896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\cdot 6^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493014} {:answer/id #uuid "b274efb9-234d-5a10-a8b5-3cd22fdb67d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6^{x}}{5 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493017} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f318d0-f1ac-5767-b6d6-5968ca82870b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\cdot 6^{\\frac{x}{5}}}{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8f318d0-f1ac-5767-b6d6-5968ca82870b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\cdot 6^{\\frac{x}{5}}}{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493019} :answer-field/id #uuid "109716d1-dff5-5cb4-b22b-82cd881dc9e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493008}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98a4bd56-0285-5f4b-b31e-2a51803f2189" :question/problem "$\\int 6^{\\frac{x}{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{5},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 5\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6^{\\frac{x}{5}}\\,\\text{d}x&=\\int 6^{u}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &=5\\int 6^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &=5\\frac{6^{u}}{\\ln 6}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{5 \\cdot 6^{\\frac{x}{5}}}{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197a6e1c-ac21-5ce4-99cd-b8ab6c5541e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6^{4 x - 5}}{4 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493009} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef814e7-9aa7-51a1-ae5f-0cb54dc801b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6^{2 x - 5}}{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493010} {:answer/id #uuid "4d43fdae-0415-589c-a452-b9e2e785202c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6^{4 x - 5}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493012} {:answer/id #uuid "98c228a9-986e-55be-8e4d-9ebb09a415b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6^{2 x - 5}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493016} {:answer/id #uuid "df0ab8b2-51a9-59dc-bd23-253d0062c1bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6^{4 x - 5}}{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "197a6e1c-ac21-5ce4-99cd-b8ab6c5541e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6^{4 x - 5}}{4 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493009} :answer-field/id #uuid "3673e5d8-318d-5d29-ac81-edbe9ff6ffb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493011}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b59c6ae3-b0c9-51c8-9fe5-f6714e9067eb" :question/problem "What is $\\int 6^{4 x - 5}\\,\\text{d}x$?" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=4 x - 5,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6^{4 x - 5}\\,\\text{d}x&=\\int 6^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{4}\\int 6^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{6^{u}}{\\ln 6}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{6^{4 x - 5}}{4 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a General Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105575 :question/id #uuid "bf47e35e-da86-5f88-b1e7-0cc731c37b31" :question/math-academy-id "e-376" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\dfrac {3+2x} {x} ?$" :question/worked-solution "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C =3 \\ln K$ where $K$ is some other constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =3ln⁡(K|x|)+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195 :topic/math-academy-id 30 :topic/title "Combining the Laws of Logarithms"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106053 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c3ac21a-faf1-5c31-bd11-bd0f1ae29c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272906} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6b6e0f-74f9-56a0-8308-d6aa4f51232e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273055} {:answer/id #uuid "69c3ed4f-390d-54e1-a838-202b4306bfb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273299} {:answer/id #uuid "be5b380d-ca11-584f-b71d-959978d30a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274036} {:answer/id #uuid "defd0299-dbd4-58d9-bf72-ce42aef07558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{8}{3}K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f6b6e0f-74f9-56a0-8308-d6aa4f51232e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273055} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb4ad755-d29b-529f-b9a7-201a6ce07910" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274990}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4863b144-6916-5476-8b2b-08b73058b19d" :question/math-academy-id "q-15717" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{8}{3x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{8}{3}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{8}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83c63472-a761-505e-84f7-9153561670c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (K(6 + 7 \\mid x \\mid))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104339} {:answer/id #uuid "d3622d1b-baef-57de-b3ad-f20fdaee0d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\ln (K \\mid x \\mid + 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104340} {:answer/id #uuid "d655eaf8-cf8a-5690-8a86-e690c9ec3e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\ln (K \\mid x \\mid) - 7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104341} {:answer/id #uuid "43f2be20-65ec-5098-b876-aec2a5f992af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (7K \\mid x \\mid + 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104342} {:answer/id #uuid "6e860cae-7bf5-5347-bdde-248d49098120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\ln (K \\mid x \\mid) + 7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104343}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b78f248e-f514-5af8-8b05-d61ee1654634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104338}] :question/id #uuid "d467f8ff-d209-5e19-931d-0d63b82b1623" :question/math-academy-id "q-49096" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{6 + 7x}{x}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35399b5c-61ff-5ca5-be4f-4c1abe0d88b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{3}{2x}+3ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236154} {:answer/id #uuid "6a533669-c40f-5a00-940e-dadb7f469489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{2x}+ln⁡(3K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236488} {:answer/id #uuid "7fce5c0b-fbff-51c0-9373-cb763d114999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{2x}+3ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236610} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5b6f3b-c7d8-5d5b-a12e-71a1116a0a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236725} {:answer/id #uuid "c8374c9a-abe6-515a-a258-f3f08a5ed775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{9}{4x}+ln⁡(3K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f5b6f3b-c7d8-5d5b-a12e-71a1116a0a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236725} :answer-field/id #uuid "356e1270-3ef3-5f47-b27e-aac16e288e4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237514}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12ca3bb3-d360-53db-af13-e44e401ba3a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-103375" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{9}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+3ln⁡|x|+\\frac{9{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+3ln⁡|x|-\\frac{9}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=3ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln⁡|x|+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fc5000e-2b32-58d7-ad16-c7de786b85e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{ln⁡(K|x|)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236225} {:answer/id #uuid "89e62549-2f57-5be7-80e7-7b01223e1dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡(\\frac{K|x|}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236681} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca66147-f622-51fe-8f8d-c945ee0a90c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236797} {:answer/id #uuid "a162783a-2e70-5f31-8790-4cae86e77c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{ln⁡(K|x|)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236827} {:answer/id #uuid "c42b65fc-6ad6-5255-ad37-78be530512d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡(2K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ca66147-f622-51fe-8f8d-c945ee0a90c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236797} :answer-field/id #uuid "684eb99d-9162-5ff2-afed-d23e9ffc201a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237574}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57af19c5-8aad-56d8-8498-0ea830a26c13" :question/math-academy-id "q-103603" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $2{x}^{-1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=2ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $2ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2ln⁡|x|+2ln⁡K \\\\  & =2(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =2ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a38b29-85ad-5374-bea2-88f31b9b11e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235933} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8e5d23-edd6-5e66-a10e-e0cae75992cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235989} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf58edf-2b25-5403-987e-544048dac46f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236402} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e0bbcf-015a-5615-9e6f-2fe0003bc2eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236976} {:answer/id #uuid "d4375fca-91f8-5c71-956b-c92f62e78b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4375fca-91f8-5c71-956b-c92f62e78b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237158} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ed20a98-5dd7-5597-b788-a5b92556c748" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237454}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de2d042e-94ce-510a-ada4-3da6c7a2612d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103605" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{2x-1}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2-\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\  & =2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =2x-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "244e842d-6715-5aeb-b13b-adc8b6cf5d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡(\\frac{5}{2}K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272706} {:answer/id #uuid "5194416d-ecec-5f21-915f-c325c30b65c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273082} {:answer/id #uuid "75ed1eed-b51b-5097-9cd3-a39f055f5c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡(\\frac{5}{2}|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273390} {:answer/id #uuid "7e002927-2ce6-5656-8e9d-e32f1ff37552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273462} {:answer/id #uuid "c988e25e-fd28-52c7-b118-2623bf44beb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡(5K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e002927-2ce6-5656-8e9d-e32f1ff37552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273462} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e9cd3aa-2595-5209-9d8a-dc52f793d875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274875}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afa04175-238c-5ba8-a826-771eab746826" :question/math-academy-id "q-15710" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{5}{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{5}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|+\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9f3ccb-57a5-54e7-9651-cd8e38e071bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡(5K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295330} {:answer/id #uuid "137963fa-e7bf-5b99-b97d-16a6e2b83b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295376} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba78d99-70e5-5576-9ab3-f6f5d63e5557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295610} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6cacd1-8561-54df-98e0-e7424109595d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-ln⁡(\\frac{5}{2}K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296168} {:answer/id #uuid "e35e2f64-0c17-5dfe-a27c-81e2452e3e98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba78d99-70e5-5576-9ab3-f6f5d63e5557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295610} :answer-field/id #uuid "c450e82f-bb3f-51ae-bbf9-78580de51634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297036}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eec65b66-7ead-5d6e-b179-5aacd453b9d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-29834" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $-\\frac{5}{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-\\frac{5}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332658 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009d8131-2ffe-5c14-a378-d1f56781bded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{1}{x}+2ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329958} {:answer/id #uuid "3c89723c-b495-521d-8ad7-e34b357b5559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{x}+2ln⁡(|K|x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330487} {:answer/id #uuid "7a074152-9317-5da0-b3eb-342d5c464ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2ln⁡(|K|x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331032} {:answer/id #uuid "a03000cf-9b4c-5aa7-bc81-3a390be00d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{1}{x}+ln⁡(2K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331391} {:answer/id #uuid "e71d945d-91e1-5744-9e87-32bc4829e8a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+(1+ln⁡(K|x|))^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "009d8131-2ffe-5c14-a378-d1f56781bded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{1}{x}+2ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329958} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f28b39-da74-5b7f-b9d6-861abb2e2dcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332321}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "edf6e555-e835-5bc6-9e0b-944e25006b84" :question/math-academy-id "q-49067" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+2ln⁡|x|+\\frac{{x}^{-2+1}}{-2+1}+C \\\\  & =x+2ln⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=2ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $2ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{1}{x}+2ln⁡|x|+2ln⁡K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{x}+2(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{x}+2ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3547442c-97cf-54cc-ab1e-894010d4f12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\fra